# Compatibility

SquirrelJME aims to be compatible with most programs however there are
situations and rare edge cases where there will be no compatibility. This is
to make the implementation easier.

## `Flushable` Interface

The interface `Flushable` does not exist in Java ME and as such it does not
exist in SquirrelJME.

## LCDUI Display Input

Only the first display (index zero) supports input events such as keyboard,
buttons, mice, and touchscreen. Any secondary displays which are attached
will not have events handled for them at all. This is to make it easier to
implement.

## Limited Network Interface Support

The Generic Connection Framework (GCF) has API to provide access to various
system interfaces, WiFi devices, 3GPP/CDMA (Mobile data), and dial-up
connections. Since supporting all of these are complicated, support for these
across systems will be limited to an as needed basis.

## `Reader`'s `lock` Is `null`

Since SquirrelJME uses a reference counted garbage collector the `Reader`'s
`lock` object in the default constructor is set to `null` instead of `this`.
This is so that readers do not remain in memory unable to be freed because
objects still refer to them and such.

## `SUPER` Is Always Set

In all clases the `SUPER` flag is assumed to be set, this flag just modifies
the behavior of `invokespecial`. When it is not set then that instruction
can invoke nearly every method directly (it was called before
`invokenonvirtual`). However, the Java compiler since Java 1.1 always set
the flag for classes it compiled. Since CLDC 1.0 was released well after
Java 2 was and is defined as a Java 2 virtual machine all code written for
J2ME should have classes where this flag is set. As such instead of
supporting the logic to handle cases where it is not set, the flag will just
be ignored and assumed to be set.

## Supported Encodings

Only the following character encodings are supported:

 * _ASCII_
 * _IBM037_
 * _ISO-8859-1_
 * _UTF-8_

Note that UTF codepoints above U+FFFF are not fully supported and are not
available in Java ME. Surrogate pairs in UTF-16 will be encoded in UTF-8 as
six bytes. Additionally, when decoding, if a sequence that is above U+FFFF is
read then it will decode to the replacement character. This may cause a loss of
or mutation of data when processing files.

See:

 * <https://unicode.org/faq/utf_bom.html#utf8-4>
 * <https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr26/>

## ScritchUI Graphics Serialization

All graphics operations in ScritchUI are serialized and any draw operations
are hard forced to occur in the graphics drawing thread. Unlike traditional
APIs for operating systems where this is intended to be done by the developer,
SquirrelJME forces this paradigm to be used. This generally fixes and
stabilizes software that performs locking and/or game logic
in `Canvas.paint()` methods, or similar.

This allows software such as _Gradius NEO_ on DoJa to run, and for the
framerate of _Ratchet and Clank_ on MIDP to be stable and not run absurdly
fast.

## Configuration/Platform Version Differences

SquirrelJME will auto-detect the version of the configuration and platform
from the manifest or a set of other indicators. This is used to determine the
version of said specific standard or device that software is made for. Without
this, the subtle changes that occur due to breaking API updates will cause
software to change behavior.

### MEEP/MIDP Version Differences

 * _>= 3.0_: The `Canvas` by default is wiped with a background color by
   default unless `setPaintMode()` changes this, previously no implicit
   clearing was performed and all pixels must be drawn. Otherwise, an
   effect called the "hall-of-mirrors" would occur, a type of smearing.
 * _< 3.0_: The alpha channel is not supported, therefor the
   method `Graphics.setAlphaColor()` will always set the color to be
   fully opaque. Accordingly, `Graphics.getAlpha()` and related will always
   have the alpha channel always set to be fully-opaque.
 * _< 8.0_: `RecordStore` does not support IDs that are recycled and as
   such all new records will always have a higher value ID regardless.
   MEEP 8 however, recycles old IDs as records no longer need to be accessed
   purely by IDs, as there are named aliases and tags.

### DoJa Version Differences 

 * _>= 5.0_: Adds support for looped MLDs and emits `AUDIO_LOOPED`, otherwise
   they will not be emitted at all. The `END_EVENT` is considered to be the 
   looping point. Otherwise, if there is no end event and the media reaches
   the end, or DoJa is not new enough, `AUDIO_COMPLETE` will be emitted.
   If `EVENT_LOOP` is hit, the MLD will jump to position zero.
 * _< 2.0_: `AudioPresenter` priority does not exist, thus all new sounds will
   always replace the already playing sound.
 * _< 3.0_: `AudioPresenter` does not support setting the volume level, thus
   all volume will be at _100%_.
 * _>= 3.0_: `AudioPresenter.setSound()` requires that any set sounds be
   `use()`ed first, otherwise it will fail.
 * _< 3.0_: `AudioPresenter` only supports a single sound at once, therefor
   SquirrelJME's dynamic port cycling will not take effect and always
   choose the first port.
 * _< 3.5_: `AudioPresenter` supports multiple ports, however when requesting
   the default port it will always a non-specific default port to mimic the
   behavior of _< 3.0_.
 * _< 4.0_: The alpha channel and alpha blending does not exist and all
   values will be stripped and forced to be fully opaque regardless of their
   input values or drawing mode.
 * _< 4.0_: Due to the lack of alpha channel and alpha blending, the values
   returned by `Graphics.getColorOfName()` and `Graphics.getColorOfRGB()`
   will always return with the uppermost bits set to zero. This must be
   considered as fully-opaque as normally a value of zero means
   fully-transparent. Otherwise, the values returned will have their
   uppermost bits set to one. 
 * _>= 2.0_: Disposing of graphics is supported, which frees associated
   resources accordingly.
 * _< 3.0_: Application suspension is not supported, thus the
   in-the-background multitasking that is provided by MIDP 3 will always
   result it in being reported as not supported. However, SquirrelJME will
   still be able to background tasks, despite the application being unable
   to receive those events nor determine if it is running in the background.
   This may cause performance issues if an application continues executing
   when it should not be doing more work than usual while the user is not
   using it.

## Configuration Settings That May Be Used For Compatibility

These settings may be used for compatibility purposes if needed:

 * `cc.squirreljme.frame=WxH` -- Set the default resolution of SquirrelJME.
   * This should be used if SquirrelJME is unable to determine the actual
     resolution that an application should use and there otherwise are
     graphical glitches or other anomalies due to this.

 * `cc.squirreljme.keymap=vendor` -- Use a vendor's keys in SquirrelJME.
   * This should be used if an application requires a specific vendor's
     keycodes in order to function properly. This is often the case with
     applications developed for Nokia, Siemens, among others.
